1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,275
Hold an autumn leaf up to the light.

2
00:00:02,275 --> 00:00:04,175
Where did that gold come from?

3
00:00:04,175 --> 00:00:05,150
All summer,

4
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:07,600
a pigment called chlorophyll made the leaf look

5
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:08,150
green.

6
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:10,175
As the leaf gets ready to fall,

7
00:00:10,175 --> 00:00:11,825
its chlorophyll breaks down.

8
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:14,550
Yellow and orange pigments were there all along.

9
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:16,300
They are called carotenoids.

10
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,675
The strong green of chlorophyll had masked those

11
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:19,500
colours.

12
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,125
Now that the green fades,

13
00:00:21,125 --> 00:00:22,125
we can see them.

14
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:23,900
Red has a different story.

15
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,400
Some trees make red pigments in autumn.

16
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,500
So an autumn canopy shows both colours uncovered

17
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:30,750
and colours newly made.

